      "writtenOn": "2022-06-13T15:21:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m surprised this has any practical effect here. I would think for buffers the only possible advantage to invalidating would be if you dynamically writing/reading data to them (and then reading) during a set of draws. And then you want to invalidate before the end of the frame so the gpu doesn\u0027t have to move that memory from some cache back to real memory.\n\nI would have thought if we\u0027re at a point of mapping the buffers for our typical usage patterns the memory would have already been written back out from some previous frame.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 220,
        "startChar": 28,
        "endLine": 220,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "71126aaea19e617416b776bf36276e4be3fdeb3f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f3e0b299_abf9393f",
        "filename": "src/gpu/ganesh/gl/GrGLBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 220,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-13T15:42:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we\u0027re mapping for write access the driver doesn\u0027t know if we\u0027re going to overwrite the entire buffer or not. So it may synchronize with GPU here if there are still reads or writes in flight on the GPU. By invalidating the driver can make a new allocation for the map and replace the buffer\u0027s storage once previous operations are complete. It\u0027s possible that we should do this invalidation earlier but we don\u0027t currently have an interface on GrGpuBuffer for that.",
      "parentUuid": "5bc65512_2918d539",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 220,
        "startChar": 28,
        "endLine": 220,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "71126aaea19e617416b776bf36276e4be3fdeb3f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
